{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1287 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "34772d1b5ce684e04a939d52577a6e2750dfd08b6a2be4a86f593eb540b8dd40" :edb/db-before-t 1286 :edb/db-after-t 1287 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534599 50 #inst "2026-10-12T04:22:42.118Z" 13194139534599 17592186045417 true] [17592186153868 1078 #edb/ref 17592186153871 13194139534599 17592186045417 true] [17592186156994 1071 "The continuous random variable $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = 2x, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq 1\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\textrm{E}[X] = \\dfrac{2}{3},$ compute $\\textrm{E}[5X^2 - 2X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $\\textrm{E}[aX + bY] = a\\textrm{E}[X] + b\\textrm{E}[Y]$ for any random variables $X$ and $Y$ and constants $a$ and $b.$*" 13194139534599 17592186045417 true] [17592186156994 1071 "The continuous random variable $X$ has the following probability density function:\n$f(x) = 2x, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq 1$\n\nGiven that $\\textrm{E}[X] = \\dfrac{2}{3}$, compute $\\textrm{E}[5X^2 - 2X]$.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $\\textrm{E}[aX + bY] = a\\textrm{E}[X] + b\\textrm{E}[Y]$ for any random variables $X$ and $Y$ and constants $a$ and $b$.*" 13194139534599 17592186045417 false] [17592186156994 1074 "First, we simplify using the hint and the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[5X^{2} - 2X] &= 5E[X^{2}] - 2E[X]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$, the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X^n] = \\int_S x^n \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nWe already know $\\textrm E [X]$. So, we compute the second raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X^{2}] &= \\int_{0}^{1}x^{2} \\cdot 2xdx \\\\\n&= 2\\int_{0}^{1}x^{3}dx \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{1}{2}x^{4}\\right|_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(1 - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X^2] = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\ninto the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[5X^{2} - 2X] &= 5E[X^{2}] - 2E[X] \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} - 2 \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{2} - \\frac{4}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534599 17592186045417 false] [17592186156994 1074 "First, we simplify using the hint and the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[5{X}^{2}-2X] & =5\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-2\\text{E}[X]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X^n] = \\int_S x^n \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe already know $\\textrm E [X].$ So, we compute the second raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\textrm{E}[X] = \\dfrac{2}{3}$ and $\\textrm{E}[X^2] = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[5{X}^{2}-2X] & =5\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-2\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534599 17592186045417 true] [17592186156995 1071 "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n${\\frac{1}{8},\\begin{vmatrix}-2 < x < 0, \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x,\\end{vmatrix}0 \\le x < 1$.\n\nCalculate $\\textrm{E}[X^2]$." 13194139534599 17592186045417 false] [17592186156995 1071 "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n![](images/f3/f3149524cf7975083139c82369095a564c5cb28e66ecd1bdab94721c64bd6d21.svg)\n\nCalculate $\\textrm{E}[X^2].$" 13194139534599 17592186045417 true] [17592186156995 1074 "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$, the expected value of $X^2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X^2] = \\int_{S} x^2 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the expected value of $X^2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X^{2}] &= \\int_{-2}^{1}x^{2} \\cdot f(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{-2}^{0}x^{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{8}dx + \\int_{0}^{1}x^{2} \\cdot \\frac{3}{2}xdx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8}\\int_{-2}^{0}x^{2}dx + \\frac{3}{2}\\int_{0}^{1}x^{3}dx \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{1}{24}x^{3}\\right|_{-2}^{0} + \\left.\\frac{3}{8}x^{4}\\right|_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{24}(0 + 8) + \\frac{3}{8}(1 - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{3}{8} \\\\\n&= \\frac{17}{24}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534599 17592186045417 false] [17592186156995 1074 "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the expected value of $X^2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X^2] = \\int_{S} x^2 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the expected value of $X^2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\,x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}\\,{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{-2}^{0}+\\frac{3}{8}\\,{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}(0+8)+\\frac{3}{8}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{3}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534599 17592186045417 true] [17592186156996 1071 "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n${1 - x,\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2},\\end{vmatrix}1 < x \\le 2$.\n\nCalculate $\\textrm E [X^4]$." 13194139534599 17592186045417 false] [17592186156996 1071 "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n![](images/19/1940f446dbc6c352b86f413501008abd7ac519a79cfa6be9b7207ecfe13609a7.svg)\n\nCalculate $\\textrm E [X^4].$" 13194139534599 17592186045417 true] [17592186156996 1074 "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$, the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X^n] = \\int_{S} x^n \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the fourth raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X^{4}] &= \\int_{0}^{2}x^{4} \\cdot f(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}x^{4} \\cdot (1 - x)dx + \\int_{1}^{2}x^{4} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}(x^{4} - x^{5})dx + \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{1}^{2}x^{4}dx \\\\\n&= \\left.(\\frac{1}{5}x^{5} - \\frac{1}{6}x^{6})\\right|_{0}^{1} + \\left.\\frac{1}{10}x^{5}\\right|_{1}^{2} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1}{5} - \\frac{1}{6} - 0) + \\frac{1}{10}(32 - 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{30} + \\frac{31}{10} \\\\\n&= \\frac{47}{15}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534599 17592186045417 false] [17592186156996 1074 "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X^n] = \\int_{S} x^n \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the fourth raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{4}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{4}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}\\cdot (1-x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{5}\\,{x}^{5}-\\frac{1}{6}\\,{x}^{6})\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+\\frac{1}{10}\\,{x}^{5}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{6}-0)+\\frac{1}{10}(32-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{30}+\\frac{31}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{47}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534599 17592186045417 true] [17592186157393 1038 #edb/ref 17592186427364 13194139534599 17592186045417 true] [17592186157393 1038 #edb/ref 17592186427367 13194139534599 17592186045417 true] [17592186157393 1038 #edb/ref 17592186427369 13194139534599 17592186045417 true] [17592186157393 1038 #edb/ref 17592186427370 13194139534599 17592186045417 true] [17592186157393 1038 #edb/ref 17592186427371 13194139534599 17592186045417 true] [17592186157394 1038 #edb/ref 17592186427360 13194139534599 17592186045417 true] [17592186157394 1038 #edb/ref 17592186427361 13194139534599 17592186045417 true] [17592186157394 1038 #edb/ref 17592186427368 13194139534599 17592186045417 true] [17592186157394 1038 #edb/ref 17592186427372 13194139534599 17592186045417 true] [17592186157394 1038 #edb/ref 17592186427373 13194139534599 17592186045417 true] [17592186157395 1038 #edb/ref 17592186427362 13194139534599 17592186045417 true] [17592186157395 1038 #edb/ref 17592186427363 13194139534599 17592186045417 true] [17592186157395 1038 #edb/ref 17592186427365 13194139534599 17592186045417 true] [17592186157395 1038 #edb/ref 17592186427366 13194139534599 17592186045417 true] [17592186157533 1050 600.0 13194139534599 17592186045417 true] [17592186157604 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534599 17592186045417 true] [17592186157604 1071 "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$f(x) = {\\frac{3}{x^{4}},\\begin{vmatrix}1 < x \\le 2 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3x^{3}},\\end{vmatrix}2 \\le x < 4$.\n\nCalculate $E[X^{4}]$." 13194139534599 17592186045417 false] [17592186157604 1071 "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}, & 1<x\\le 2 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3{x}^{3}}, & 2\\le x<4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{4}].$" 13194139534599 17592186045417 true] [17592186157604 1074 "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the fourth raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{4}] & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{x}^{4}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}{x}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{4}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{4}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3x\\,{|}_{1}^{2}+{\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{2}|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =3(2-1)+\\frac{2}{3}(16-4) \\\\  & =3+8 \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534599 17592186045417 true] [17592186158562 1064 "Suppose $X$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$.\n\nThe values $\\textrm{E}[X]$ and $\\textrm{E}[X^2]$ are called the **first moment** and **second moment** of $X$, respectively.\n\nRecall that the first moment $\\textrm{E}[X]$ is also called the *expected value* of $X$ and is computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X] = \\int_S x \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nTo find second moment $\\textrm{E}[X^2]$, the expected value of the the random variable $X^2$, we simply substitute $x^2$ for $x$ in the formula above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X^2] = \\int_S x^2 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nLet's compute the second moment $\\textrm{E}[X^2]$ of the continuous random variable $X$ with the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}, \\qquad 0 \\leq x \\leq 1\n$$\n\nWe compute the expected value of $X^2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X^{2}] &= \\int_{S}x^{2} \\cdot f(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}x^{2} \\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}\\int_{0}^{1}x^{5/2}dx \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{2}{7}x^{7/2})\\right|_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{2}{7} - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534599 17592186045417 false] [17592186158562 1064 "Suppose $X$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S.$\n\nThe values $\\textrm{E}[X]$ and $\\textrm{E}[X^2]$ are called the **first moment** and **second moment** of $X,$ respectively. \n\nRecall that the first moment $\\textrm{E}[X]$ is also called the *expected value* of $X$ and is computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X] = \\int_S x \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nTo find second moment $\\textrm{E}[X^2],$ the expected value of the the random variable $X^2,$ we simply substitute $x^2$ for $x$ in the formula above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X^2] = \\int_S x^2 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nLet's compute the second moment $\\textrm{E}[X^2]$ of the continuous random variable $X$ with the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}, \\qquad 0 \\leq x \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nWe compute the expected value of $X^2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{5/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{2}{7}{x}^{7/2}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{2}{7}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534599 17592186045417 true] [17592186158563 1064 "It's also possible to define higher moments. For example, $\\textrm{E}[X^3]$ is the **third moment** of the random variable $X$.\n\nNotice the pattern:\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X] = \\displaystyle \\int_S x \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x$ is the *first* moment\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X^2] = \\displaystyle \\int_S x^2 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x$ is the *second* moment\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X^3] = \\displaystyle \\int_S x^3 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x$ is the *third* moment\n\nand so on. In general, the **$n$th moment** is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X^n] = \\int_S x^n \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFinally, note that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X], \\quad \\textrm{E}[X^2], \\quad \\textrm{E}[X^3], \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nare sometimes referred to as the first, second, and third **raw moments**. This is because it's possible to define other types of moments.\n\nIn general, $\\textrm{E}[X^n]$ can be called either the $n$th *moment* or $n$th *raw moment*." 13194139534599 17592186045417 false] [17592186158563 1064 "It's also possible to define higher moments. For example, $\\textrm{E}[X^3]$ is the **third moment** of the random variable $X.$\n\nNotice the pattern:\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X] = \\int_S x \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x$ is the *first* moment\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X^2] = \\int_S x^2 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x$ is the *second* moment\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X^3] = \\int_S x^3 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x$ is the *third* moment\n\nand so on. In general, the **$n$th moment** is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X^n] = \\int_S x^n \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, note that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X], \\quad \\textrm{E}[X^2], \\quad \\textrm{E}[X^3], \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nare sometimes referred to as the first, second, and third **raw moments**. This is because it's possible to define other types of moments.\n\nIn general, $\\textrm{E}[X^n]$ can be called either the $n$th *moment* or $n$th *raw moment*." 13194139534599 17592186045417 true] [17592186427276 1080 "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2}" 13194139534599 17592186045417 true] [17592186427276 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534599 17592186045417 true] [17592186427276 1083 #uuid "00ec450b-8c39-502c-a433-fd5a4ae4c4b0" 13194139534599 17592186045417 true] [17592186427277 1080 "\\frac{53}{10}" 13194139534599 17592186045417 true] [17592186427277 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534599 17592186045417 true] [17592186427277 1083 #uuid "044244e8-f2a2-5ef1-8833-22499068eac4" 13194139534599 17592186045417 true] [17592186427278 1080 "\\frac{29}{7}" 13194139534599 17592186045417 true] [17592186427278 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534599 17592186045417 true] [17592186427278 1083 #uuid "09fdd550-bf28-5f33-af90-7628e13259a1" 13194139534599 17592186045417 true] [17592186427279 1